{"id":"57f28fde-24f3-4987-8853-e4358b01e2e0","object":"chat.completion","created":1784835093,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\\\\n        raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"Invalid case: missing required keys\\\\\\\")\\\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\\\n    calls = case['calls']\\\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\\\n        raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"Invalid case: calls must be a list\\\\\\\")\\\\n    accepted = []\\\\n    diagnostics = []\\\\n    for call in calls:\\\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\\\n            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"Invalid call: not a dict\\\\\\\")\\\\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\\\\n        for key in required:\\\\n            if key not in call:\\\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\\\\\"Missing field {key} in call\\\\\\\")\\\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\\\n        pred_tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\\\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\\\\n            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"loss_without_call must be numeric\\\\\\\")\\\\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\\\\n            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"loss_with_call must be numeric\\\\\\\")\\\\n        if not isinstance(pred_tokens, int) or pred_tokens < 1:\\\\n            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"prediction_tokens must be positive integer\\\\\\\")\\\\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\\\\n            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"parseable must be boolean\\\\\\\")\\\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\\\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\\\\n        accepted_flag = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\\\\n        gain_rounded = round(gain, 8)\\\\n        norm_gain_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 8)\\\\n        diagnostics.append({\\\\n            'call_id': call_id,\\\\n            'gain': gain_rounded,\\\\n            'normalized_gain': norm_gain_rounded,\\\\n            'accepted': accepted_flag\\\\n        })\\\\n        if accepted_flag:\\\\n            accepted.append(call_id)\\\\n    return {\\\\n        'accepted': accepted,\\\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is Python source code defining solve(case). The problem: implement api_call_loss_filter based on the description. The case payload has threshold and calls. Each call has call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. Compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. Normalize gain by dividing by prediction_tokens (at least one token). Call that normalized_gain. Then filter: keep calls where parseable is True and normalized_gain >= threshold. Return a dict with accepted call IDs (list) and per-call diagnostics? The description says \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" The diagnostics likely include the computed normalized_gain and maybe gain? Also \"normalized_weights\" might refer to the normalized_gain? \"stronger_counterfactual\" could be the gain? Actually \"stronger_counterfactual\" is likely the condition that we use the difference (without - with). \"inclusive_threshold\" means >= threshold. \"stable_order\" means preserve input order. So output should be something like: { \"accepted\": [\"call0\", ...], \"diagnostics\": [ {\"call_id\": ..., \"gain\": ..., \"normalized_gain\": ...}, ... ] } but need to check the specification. It says \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" and \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\". Also \"compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions\". So likely we return a dict with \"accepted\" list and \"diagnostics\" list. The diagnostics should include the computed values rounded appropriately. Also need to handle rounding: maybe to some precision? Not specified exactly; maybe to 8 decimal places as in input? The input numbers have many decimals. Could round to 8 decimals or keep as floats? Usually in such tasks they expect rounding to maybe 6 or 8. The description says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". So we must round. Let's look at the provided fixtures - they have numbers like 1.94835097. So we can round to 8 decimal places. Or we can use round(x, 8). Also need to handle case where prediction_tokens is 0? But specification says \"at least one prediction token\", so we can assume >0. Also need to validate: if any required field missing, raise ValueError. The case has threshold and calls list. Each call must have those fields. We'll validate.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Check case is dict with 'threshold' (float) and 'calls' (list).\n- For each call, check it has 'call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable'.\n- Compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call.\n- normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens.\n- If parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold, add to accepted list. Also build diagnostics dict with call_id, gain (rounded), normalized_gain (rounded).\n- Return dict with keys: 'accepted' (list of call_ids in order), 'diagnostics' (list of dicts in same order, one per call, including both accepted and rejected? Or only accepted? The phrase \"per-call diagnostics\" suggests for all calls? Usually diagnostics for each call to audit decision. But \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" could mean return diagnostics for the accepted calls. However \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" suggests we might want to include all calls? Let's read carefully: \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" That implies diagnostics for all calls, not just accepted. Because if only accepted, order is a subset. Also \"compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" So we return diagnostics for each call, then the aggregate (like count of accepted?) But we are not asked to compute aggregate, just return accepted IDs and diagnostics. So likely diagnostics for all calls, with fields like call_id, gain, normalized_gain, accepted? Or we could include a boolean 'accepted' field. The specification doesn't specify exact fields. But we can infer from typical patterns: they want to retain the per-call diagnostics that justify the decision. So include call_id, gain, normalized_gain, and maybe accepted flag. But we need to be consistent with \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". We'll assume we need to output for each call (in order) the computed values. The accepted list is separate.\n\nLet's define output shape:\n{\n  \"accepted\": [list of call_ids],\n  \"diagnostics\": [\n    {\"call_id\": \"...\", \"gain\": ..., \"normalized_gain\": ..., \"accepted\": bool},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\nBut we also need to round gain and normalized_gain. Use round(..., 8). Or we could use a fixed rounding as per typical float representation. The inputs have up to 8 decimal places, so 8 is reasonable.\n\nIf prediction_tokens is 0, we should treat as invalid? But specification says \"at least one prediction token\", so if 0, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso need to handle the case where parseable is not boolean? It's given as true/false in JSON, but in Python it becomes bool. So fine.\n\nFinally, the function solve(case) must return JSON-serializable object. So ensure no non-serializable types.\n\nWe'll write the code with proper validation. Use isinstance checks.\n\nAlso note: The task says \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: Toolformer API-call filtering with supplied token losses; call generation and language-model training are excluded.\" So we only do filtering.\n\nNo external imports needed.\n\nWrite code, then escape for JSON string.\n\nLet's produce the Python source as a single string in the JSON.\n\nWe need to ensure the JSON is valid. Use triple-quoted string? But JSON requires escaping newlines and quotes. We'll just write the code as a single line with \\n for newlines. Or we can use a multiline string in Python but for JSON we need to escape. We'll do it programmatically? But we are writing manually. We'll produce the code with proper escapes.\n\nBetter to output the code as a string with embedded newlines escaped as \\n and quotes escaped as \\\". Also backslashes need escaping. We'll generate the JSON object manually.\n\nFirst, write the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case structure\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing required keys\")\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: calls must be a list\")\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid call: not a dict\")\n        # Validate required fields\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\n        for key in required:\n            if key not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Missing field {key} in call\")\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        pred_tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        # Validate types\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_without_call must be numeric\")\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_with_call must be numeric\")\n        if not isinstance(pred_tokens, int) or pred_tokens < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"prediction_tokens must be positive integer\")\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"parseable must be boolean\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\n        accepted_flag = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        # Round to 8 decimal places\n        gain_rounded = round(gain, 8)\n        norm_gain_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 8)\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'gain': gain_rounded,\n            'normalized_gain': norm_gain_rounded,\n            'accepted': accepted_flag\n        })\n        if accepted_flag:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        'accepted': accepted,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nNow we need to format this as a JSON string. Replace newlines with \\n, backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\". Also ensure indentation is minimal? We can minify or keep as is. But the entire response must be one JSON object. So we will wrap in {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. We'll escape the code.\n\nLet's write the code as a single line with \\n. We'll do it carefully.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a Python script to generate the JSON, but we are in text. I'll manually escape.\n\nI'll write the code with escapes:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate case structure\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: missing required keys\\\")\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: calls must be a list\\\")\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid call: not a dict\\\")\\n        requi